      "label": "01 · Foundations",
      "color": "3"
    },
    {
      "id": "0be7189c4ba6e8eb",
      "type": "group",
      "x": 680,
      "y": 520,
      "width": 520,
      "height": 1010,
      "label": "02 · Mechanism",
      "color": "5"
    },
    {
      "id": "91dfbf58fdf6fdef",
      "type": "group",
      "x": 1360,
      "y": 520,
      "width": 520,
      "height": 1040,
      "label": "03 · Application and limits",
      "color": "1"
    },
    {
      "id": "d10509526e7d9966",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "width": 1040,
      "height": 390,
      "text": "<!-- recall-map: overview -->\n# One-minute recall\n**Central question:** How does a system use feedback to regulate its behavior?\n**Answer:** A feedback system compares observed behavior with a target and uses the difference to adjust its next action.\n\n**Takeaways**\n- Feedback closes the loop between an action and its observed effect.\n- The sign and timing of the response determine whether behavior stabilizes or amplifies.\n- Useful control requires a measurable target, an observation, and an adjustment rule.",
      "color": "6"
    },
    {
      "id": "32ee698d12711e52",
      "type": "file",
      "x": 1120,
      "y": 0,
      "width": 420,
      "height": 390,
      "file": "sample.md",
      "color": "1"
    },
    {
      "id": "2ee15308da2a8dbb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 610,
      "width": 440,
      "height": 350,
      "text": "<!-- recall-map: concept -->\n<!-- recall-kind: foundation -->\n### Target state\nThe target states the behavior the system is trying to maintain or reach.\n\n- It must be defined in a measurable form.\n\n[[sample.md#Core components|Source p.1]]",
      "color": "3"
    },
    {
      "id": "37a1be0973adc625",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 1030,
      "width": 440,
      "height": 380,
      "text": "<!-- recall-map: concept -->\n<!-- recall-kind: concept -->\n### Observation\nThe observation measures the system's current behavior so it can be compared with the target.\n\n- Measurement delay and noise affect the comparison.\n\n[[sample.md#Core components|Source p.1]]",
      "color": "3"
    },
    {
      "id": "9a9aa19856c550d1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 720,
      "y": 610,
      "width": 440,
      "height": 380,
      "text": "<!-- recall-map: concept -->\n<!-- recall-kind: mechanism -->\n### Error signal\nThe difference between target and observation becomes the signal that drives correction.\n\n- Its sign determines the direction of adjustment.\n\n[[sample.md#How feedback works|Source p.2]]",
      "color": "5"
    },
    {
      "id": "c212db37ae5e5d7f",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 720,
      "y": 1060,
      "width": 440,
      "height": 380,
      "text": "<!-- recall-map: concept -->\n<!-- recall-kind: process -->\n### Adjustment\nThe controller converts the error signal into an action that changes the system state.\n\n- Gain determines how strongly the controller responds.\n\n[[sample.md#How feedback works|Source p.2]]",
      "color": "5"
    },
    {
      "id": "9f13a0f94faa20bc",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1400,
      "y": 610,
      "width": 440,
      "height": 380,
      "text": "<!-- recall-map: concept -->\n<!-- recall-kind: application -->\n### Stable regulation\nA well-tuned negative-feedback loop reduces deviations and settles near the target.\n\n- Small corrections prevent persistent overshoot.\n\n[[sample.md#Applications and limits|Source p.2]]",
      "color": "4"
    },
    {
      "id": "e57d06c7042efb54",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1400,
      "y": 1060,
      "width": 440,
      "height": 410,
      "text": "<!-- recall-map: concept -->\n<!-- recall-kind: boundary -->\n### Delay as a boundary\nLong delay can make a correction arrive after conditions have changed, causing oscillation or instability.\n\n- More aggressive adjustment can worsen delayed feedback.\n\n[[sample.md#Applications and limits|Source p.2]]",
      "color": "1"
    },
    {
      "id": "4f3455fad0840587",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1740,
      "width": 586,
      "height": 280,
      "text": "<!-- recall-map: synthesis -->\n# Logic chain\n1. Define the target behavior.\n2. Observe the current behavior.\n3. Calculate the difference between target and observation.\n4. Apply a correction and observe its next effect.",
      "color": "5"
    },
    {
      "id": "57b4533d2dc8f722",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 646,
      "y": 1740,
      "width": 586,
      "height": 260,
      "text": "<!-- recall-map: distinctions -->\n# Distinctions and boundaries\n- **Negative vs positive feedback:** Negative feedback opposes deviation; positive feedback reinforces the direction of change.",
      "color": "1"
    },
    {
      "id": "a8e8e28bf168775f",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1292,
      "y": 1740,
      "width": 586,
      "height": 260,
      "text": "<!-- recall-map: prompts -->\n# Active recall\n- Name the minimum components of a closed feedback loop.\n- Explain why delay can destabilize an otherwise corrective loop.\n- Predict what happens when adjustment gain is too high.",
      "color": "2"
    }
  ],
